=> Pipe | in Linux

The pipe symbol | is used in Linux to connect the output of one command to the input of another command.

Simple meaning

Think of a pipe like a pipeline:

Command 1 → | → Command 2

The output produced by Command 1 is directly given as input to Command 2.

1. Basic example
ls | less

Here:

ls → displays files and directories
| → sends the output of ls
less → displays that output page by page

So:

ls output
    ↓
    |
    ↓
less

This is useful when the output is very long.

ps + grep

One of the most common real-world examples:

2. ps aux | grep nginx

What happens?

ps aux shows all running processes.

Instead of looking through hundreds of processes manually, we pipe the output to grep.

ps aux
  ↓
  |
  ↓
grep nginx
  ↓
Only lines containing "nginx"

So the command helps us find whether nginx processes are running.

3. ls + grep
ls -l | grep ".log"

This displays files whose names contain .log.

Example:

app.log
error.log
test.txt
data.log

The pipe sends the output of ls -l to grep.

4. Multiple pipes

You can use more than one pipe.

Example:

ps aux | grep nginx | wc -l

Here:

ps aux
  ↓
grep nginx
  ↓
wc -l

=> Explaination:+

ps aux → shows processes
grep nginx → filters nginx processes
wc -l → counts the lines

So you can use pipes to create a chain of commands.

5. Pipe vs >

This is very important.

Pipe |
ls | grep txt

Means:

Send the output of ls to another command (grep).

Redirect >
ls > files.txt

Means:

Save the output of ls into a file.

So:

|  → command to command
>  → command to file

6. Real Linux Admin Example

Suppose you want to check whether a service is running:

systemctl status ssh | grep Active

Instead of viewing the complete systemctl status output, you filter it and see the line containing Active.

Another example:

df -h | grep "/"

This can help you quickly find the filesystem information for /.
